WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.990
In this video, you raise the overcommit ratios in SUSE Virtualization and add tiny virtual machines in batches of ten until a single host refuses to take more.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:18.910
In Advanced, Settings, overcommit-config sets how much CPU, memory, and storage the scheduler may promise beyond physical capacity.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.500
Open its action menu and select Edit Setting to change the defaults.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.000
For this demo, raise CPU from one thousand to two thousand percent,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.920
and memory from one hundred fifty to four hundred percent, so each small virtual machine reserves very little.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.900
Select Save to apply the new ratios.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.840
Now go to Virtual Machines and select Create.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.030
Select Multiple Instance to create several virtual machines from one form.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.200
Choose the demo namespace,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.350
the name prefix demo-dense,

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.620
and a count of ten, the most the form creates at once.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.370
Give each virtual machine one CPU

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.870
and one gigabyte of memory.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.680
On the Volumes tab,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:16.380
select the small SLES sixteen minimal image. Its LVM thin storage clones each root disk from a snapshot in seconds.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.950
The Networks tab keeps the default management network, so no addresses come from the lab DHCP pool.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:29.440
Select Create. The form creates the ten virtual machines one after another.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.350
The first ten virtual machines are running.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:38.520
Repeat the form for batch two, with the prefix demo-dense-b and the same tiny settings.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:03.720
Twenty virtual machines are running. From here on, the video cuts between batches.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:12.030
Cut. Two more batches of ten, created off camera with the same settings: forty virtual machines are running.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:19.370
Cut again. After two more batches, sixty small virtual machines are running on this one host.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:25.240
Cut. In the seventh batch, eight virtual machines start, but the host refuses the other two.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:32.180
Their pods are rejected for too many pods. Each virtual machine runs in its own pod, and the host allows two hundred.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:40.590
The final count is sixty-eight running virtual machines. The pod limit, not CPU or memory, stopped this host.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:50.801
Overcommit lets a host admit many small virtual machines, at the cost of contention under load. Check the pod limit too, and restore the default ratios when you finish testing.
